The Chamber of Deputies supported a larger share of the state from coal charges.
The Czech Republic receives more than $400 million.
The Chamber of Deputies at the first reading supported a governmental amendment to the Mining Act, which regulates the amount of fees for surface mining of brown coal from mining companies.
Minister Jan Mládek (ČSSD) vowed to deputies that thanks to this they will get both the state treasury and the budgets of the municipalities.
The bill was criticized by opposition deputies, who are afraid that mining companies will no longer want to sponsor municipalities as they have so far, because they will pay higher fees directly to the state budget.The Chamber of Deputies at first reading supported a government amendment to the Mining Act, which, among other things, means that the state should receive a larger part of the proceeds of fees from surface mining of brown coal from a mining company.
The share of the state will rise to 67 percent, while the share of municipalities will decrease from the current 75 per cent to 33 per cent.
The draft also changes the method of calculating the reimbursement for non-growths, which are mined by companies.
The change in mineral extraction fees is expected to bring 427 million crowns more to the Treasury than in 2013.
According to the government, the income of municipal budgets should have a neutral impact, because the fee should be higher.
In the year before this year, municipalities received about 450 million crowns from the fees and a state budget of CZK 150 million.
From brown coal mining, about 200 million kroner and 66 million kroner went to the municipality and 66 million kroner.
The amendment also envisages an increase in the reimbursement from mining with permitted mining to 1000 crowns for one hectare per year.
Receipt from such reimbursements should continue to belong in its entirety to municipalities in its entirety.
Minister of Industry Jan Mládek (ČSSD) told deputies that thanks to this, thanks to this, municipalities will receive between 14 million and 100 million crowns each year.
The amount of mining fees will no longer be determined by the law, but it will be determined by the government by its regulation.
Repayment is re-curably calculated as the co-measure of the amount mined non-strength and the rate for individual types of minerals.
According to the government, the reason for the change is, among other things, the possibility of a flexible response to the current economic situation.
ODS MEP Marek Benda, however, has warned of the fact that setting a fee by a government decree does not have to be in accordance with the constitution.
According to Minister Mládek, however, in this case, it is not a matter of tax, because the state is charging the mineral, which is a state property.
Fees are given only to municipalities whose territory is affected by mining.
In the case of deep-secret coal mining, three-square-threeths of the municipality's money and a quarter will be spent by the state.
There will also be the same in the case of the extraction of radioactive rocks.
Part of the money that the state collects on mining fees will be purpose-focusedly tied.
For example, 28 per cent will have to go to relieve damages after mining or to provide a mining emergency service.
The draft was criticized by opposition MPs, who argued in particular that they would harm the municipalities affected by mining and the regions.
For example, Michal Kučera (TOP 09 and the Mayors) warned that mining companies will no longer want to sponsor municipalities as they have so far, because they will pay higher fees directly to the state budget.
"It is not possible to tunnel one of the deprivated regions just for the Minister of Ex-Financials to improve the budget," said David Kádner (Úsvit) elected in the Ústek region.
KSČM MP Hana Aulická warned against the increase in unemployment in the Most region.
Kádner and Aulická unsuccessfully proposed to reject the amendment, Kučera wanted to return the law to the government for completion.
The explanatory memorandum states that, according to mining companies, an increase in fees will not be reflected in the prices of coal, because the prices from more than 90 per cent have been negotiated in the long term.
Therefore, main consumers, such as power plants or heating plants, should not feel the rise in prices.
"On the other hand, however, this fact increases the risk that mining organizations will offset the increase of reimbursements by efforts to reduce their operating expenses, which would primarily mean a reduction in the number of employees," the government admits.
According to her, however, an increase in employment fees is viable.
According to Rapporteur on the amendment Milan Urban (ČSSD), it is crucial that MPs know the conclusions about the updated energy concept and, above all, the government’s decision on coal-creation limits before passing the law.
If coal is mined even beyond the limits, then it will have an impact on revenues for the state and municipalities, he stressed. He stressed.
Urban does not agree with Mládek that the decision to break the limit on the mine of the Czechoslovak army should fall only after 2020.
According to him, the limits at the CSA mine should be breached already due to social, national economic reasons and due to the long-term stability of the domestic energy sector.
