King Salman stated today that Binadin Group should not leave the crane's shoulder at the top when it wasn't in operation.
An Arabic version of the Decree, carried by a state-of-court-led Saudi news agency, states that Binladin Group is partly to blame for the crash.
The agency's intelligence agency did not report this detail, but said the company's bosses were banned from travelling abroad.
Earlier this week, Salman vowed to reveal what caused the crane to fall into the Great Mosquito courtyard, where hundreds of thousands of Muslims co-engage in front of a Haj pilgrimage later this month.
Binladin Group did not issue a statement on the collapsing crane and its representatives were not made available for comment.
Royal ordinances have also blamed the group for the non-use of the latest safety measures, and the lack of coordination with meteorological officials.
Dozens of cranes surround Grand Mosque in Mecca, which is part of a massive construction effort led by the Binladin Group.
Binladin's family has been close to the Saudi ruling family for decades, running large construction projects.
Late al-Qaeda leader Osama bin Ládin was a renegade a renegade the family renounced in the 1990s.
The raven occurred in the midst of strong winds and rain just days before millions of people in the kingdom on a pilgrimage that every proficious Muslim must complete at least once in his life.
Officials say 158 people still remain in the hospital.
Officials have stipulated that one million riyals (£ 174,000) will be donated to the relatives of the sausages and the same amount for the permanently injured.
Other injuries will receive half this amount.
B&Q chief says Eastern European businessmen working for off-bearers already a trend
Britons increasingly employ craftsmen, Veronique Laury says
The number of trained traders from Eastern Europe is beyond the point of growth
Result is an open 200 Screwfix trade deals aimed at traders
