If you're good with a hammer or a paintbrush, you seem to be in the minority these days.
Because Britons are increasingly preoccupieding artisans to carry DIY work at their home, according to the company's boss for Screwfix B&Q.
Veronique Laury, Kingfisher's new executive who owns a home-to-equity chain, argues that a large increase in the number of educated retailers from Eastern Europe offering services at the lowest-parable prices is no longer a trend.
As a result, Kingfisher plans to unlease 200 additional screwfix trade-focused stores and closes 60 B&Q outlets.
Mrs. Laury argues that she wants to employ more women in B&Q stores, because eight out of ten shopping decisions in the store are doing better halves.
At the same time, the majority are male workers.
She said there is a need to address the growing demographic trends that show more women's households, which make more domestic adjustments than in the past.
Mrs. Laury says: "Muges are still still working in our stores.
We need to get closer to equilibrium.
Basically, half of our customers are women who carry out an 80% decision on the purchase of goods.
Women play a big role in the decision-making process.
Lady Laury, who is one of only five women at the helm of leading companies according to the British FTSE100 index, also restructured her top-of-the-line team to reflect equivaluence among men's directors.
Kingfisher stands almost alone in having a half-reservominance of women among employees, including the Executive and Chief Financial Officer.
Laury argues: "It was a conscious decision - diversity is an important issue here.
So it wasn't difficult to find the good women we'd take to us.
Kingfisher announced a slump in both mid-speaking profit and sales after the group took the cost of shutting up the shops.
Result of the Kingfisher trend, which owns Screwfix and B&Q, is to open 200 new stores
The December contribution of 600 crowns to pensioners is in jeopardy.
"It's the justice of equal stomachs, it doesn't have anything to do with human justice," said Miroslav Kalousek, vice-chairman of TOP 09, emphasizing the principle of merit.
Zbyněk Stanjura, the chairman of ODS deputies, remarked that a one-time contribution sounds good, but in the long term it would have been better for him to be in standard valorization, because it would be reflected in the next few years.
"We've got room to find the mechanism we're going to do," Stanjura said.
He supported the original proposal of Minister Michaela Marksová (ČSSD), according to which it was supposed to valorize beyond the law next year.
Marksová defended the subsidy by arguing that it would improve the social situation of retirees, which she said is not rosy.
A higher valorization next year would mean a budgetary impact of 5.5 billion crowns and ministers are debating every hundreds of a million.
Prime Minister Bohuslav Sobotka (ČSSD) reminded the right that in the past the valorization declined.
Rapporteur Miroslav Opálka (KSČM) pointed out that the proposal does not address the reduction of real pensions in 2016 and later.
The veto right will significantly complicate the payout in December and perhaps even make it impossible, he said.
Roman Sklenák has therefore proposed a shortening of the deadline for discussing the standard in the committee for ten or 20 days, even the right blocked it.
However, the committee will have a month instead of the usual two.
The bonus should be worth a total of 1.73 billion crowns.
The money should come mainly from the budget of the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs.
In the next year, pensions are expected to rise by about a hundred-crown on average.
According to the pension amendment, the contribution should be given to people who will be entitled to a pension or a part of it in December.
It could also be obtained by those whose entitlement to a retirement in December before it was redempted.
However, they would have to apply for a surcharge in writing by the end of next year at the latest.
Retirements are valorized regularly in the Czech Republic as far as January, with an increase in prices and a one-thousandth growth in real wages.
Earlier this year's retire is 41.5 per cent of the gross wage, next year it should be 40.5 per cent.
According to the state's closing account, the state spent 314.7 billion crowns on old-age pensions last year, which was about five billion more year-on-year.
Expenditure on old-age pensions accounted for 26 percent of all budget expenses and 46 per cent of all statutory expenses.
The average full old-age pension last year, according to data in the state closing account, increased by CZK 10.714 to CZK 10.714.
The Czech Societal Security Administration provided the payment of 2.4 million full old-age pensions and about 17,000 proportional pensions.
