Multiple sclerosis: it depends not only on the drugs, but also on the patient's will to fight.
Multiple sclerosis is a complicated disease and a person with this diagnosis needs the care of a number of specialists: a neurologist, a physiotherapist, ergototherapist, a urologist, ophthalmologist, a psychologist, a psychiatrist and many other specialists.
However, it is not only about therapeutic care, for example, women with multiple sclerosis also need consultations if they decide to have a child, the care of these patients also includes social and legal counseling, recommendations when regulating housing, etc.
Nevertheless, all this is not enough: the development of the disease can fundamentally affect the patient himself.
The more it will actively fight the disease, the longer it can work and does not have to fundamentally change the style of his life.
As the doctors point out, the passivity of the patients releases space for the activity of the disease.
It is proven, for example, that regular exercise significantly delays the time when the patient needs some compensatory aid, such as a stick.
It is important that patients and their surroundings do not succumb to various myths, for example, that the disease automatically leads them to end up in a wheelchair.
That’s also why we’ve created your chance (www.bezmytu.cz), which is supported by an on-the-following media campaign.
The most important person in the care of a patient with multiple sclerosis is a neurologist.
For multiple sclerositis, the adage is twice that the sooner the disease begins to be healed, the more likely the doctor can influence something.
It's because we have drugs for millennative sclerosis in an inflammatory stage, when the neurodegeneration process isn't so strong.
The sooner we intervene, the higher the hope that we will be able to stop the disease and delay the disability, explains Eva Medová from the Neurological Clinic of the General University Hospital in Prague.
It is very important in people with multiple sclerosis of rehabilitation care, which most often deals with the weakening of the lower extremities or balance disorders.
Patients are worked with according to their individual needs: to maintain fitness, improve gait, strengthen the pelvic floor, alleviate slimming disorders or to improve coordination.
"It's really individual, it depends on the specific problems of the patient," says Martina Kövári, head of the Department of Rehabilitation and Physical Education of the Motol University Hospital.
People with multiple sclerososis suffer from the same disease, it manifests itself in a slightly different way for everyone.
Rehabilitation can not stop the disease, but it can always alleviate its symptoms.
Musculoskear activity, however, also strengthens the immune state of the body, which is desirable in the case of multiple sclerositis.
Another positive of regular exercise is the fact that patients verify what they can do, and this strengthens their self-confidence.
"During the exercise, endorphins are also flushed out and a person is feeling well after him," says Kamila Řásová of the Department of Rehabilitation Medicine of the University Hospital Královské Vinohrady.
By influencing the physical side, we act on the limbic system responsible for emotions.
We directly interfere with the psyche and immune processes and stabilise the disease, he adds.
"Pregnantly, these patients were so-called saving, today we know that they need a targeted load," says Pavel Kolář, head of the Department of Rehabilitation and Physical Education at the Motol University Hospital.
Physiotherapy aims so that a person can stay self-sufficient for as long as possible, including job classification.
physiotherapists therefore work with ergotherapeuties in order to get compensatory aid tailored to them.
Numerous patients also need the care of a psychologist or a psychiatrist.
They are in a difficult life situation.
When a serious illness arrives in your life, it usually shocks people.
They're experiencing shock, panic, fear.
They have to respond to a great burden - loss of health.
With such an aching soul we are supposed to go to someone, not to try to bear everything on our own shoulders.
"I work with them in such a way that they eventually come to the conclusion that a full-fledged life can still be lived with a disability and that multiple sclerosis does not imply an end to life," says psychotherapist Renata Malinová - Schubertová from the Neurology Clinic of the General University Hospital in Prague.
Without the Prague-based Latecoere, airbuses wouldn't fly.
Aeronautical production is synonymous with precision and advanced technologies.
New aircraft are full of titanium or light and solid composite materials, which someone can not immediately produce and process.
Nevertheless, in addition to super-precise, computer-controlled machine-driven machine-storage, in addition to autoclaves for shaping composites can come across something as ordinary as apricot stones.
In a drum that commemorates a pot for cotton wool, the edges of sheet metal parts that have just come out of the milling machine are tumbling in a pile.
"So far, no one has found anything better," smiles Ivan Dubský, CEO of Air Door Producer Latecoe Czech Republic.
In the area in Letňany, the former Letov produces a complete door for Airbus A320.
It's the only one in the world to deliver the door for the Brazilian Embraer 170.
Doors, part of which are assembled in Prague, passengers also enter the first and higher class on the upper decks of the world's largest airliner Airbus A380.
There will also be doors full of philosophies for the new Boeing 787 Dreamliner.
Accuration is the main motto.
For example, momentary keys will be adjusted twice in a year.
If there is no record of the inspection on the yellow sticker, they would have something to explain to the auditors from the Airbus or Boeing, who regularly go to find out if the doors are produced according to the agreed rules.
However, advanced technology has not yet completely replaced the human senses.
There are 1500 rivets on one door to the A320.
The riveters have to shudder manually.
It takes them two days.
Even according to the sound-catching hammer, they will know if the rhyme is already trembling correctly.
Tolerance in which they move is minimal.
Rnýt can rise above the surface of the aircraft from zero to 0.13 millimeters.
That's roughly double the thickness of the human hair.
During the inspection, it will be time for a trained touch.
When the controller swipes a series of cribs, he can tell if he's out of tolerance.
Only then will he take a precise permissible radiator.
Letov nurtured the L-39 program before 1989.
There was not a single one in 1991.
The company quickly had to find new orders.
That's when it came together with European aircraft manufacturer Airbus.
She has won a tender for an emergency door supplier for A321.
Negotiations with Boeing weren't that happy.
“We were a small company that didn’t have a lot of money for the investment at the time and could guarantee Boeing’s long-term stable deliveries,” Dubsky recalled.
The door to the American manufacturer would remain slammed if Letov did not occupy the French band Latecoere at the Prague conference of the international aviation industry association in 1999.
I've been discussing our cooperation with Airbus.
Negotiations with the French began the second day, Dubsky says.
A year later Latecoe bought the Czech company and new orders came with a strong company.
Immediately in the year of 2000, the installation was carried out by parts of doors for the A320.
Threise years later there was an upper-than-range door for the A380.
In 2006, the company erected a new collaborative-settling hall due to supplies for Boeing Dreamliner.
We wouldn't be able to do these big jobs without Latecoer.
Maybe we'd do parts or small parts.
The partners want a guarantee of deliveries for twenty or thirty years, Dubský explains.
Last year, Czech Latecoere achieved a turnover of more than CZK 2.9 billion and an after-acomp collection of CZK 147 million.
An exclusive position among suppliers of aircraft manufacturers is not free of charge.
Latecoere has invested 1.5 billion crowns in Prague since 2000.
The company must have quality certificates, certification of 170 special production processes and qualification certificates for key employees.
Without them, the company wouldn't have produced a plane door.
Acquisition of certificates that the production processes meet the requirements of our customers and in repetition inspections it costs millions of crowns, according to Dubský.
The production processes change every year.
They are more accurate and stricter.
We have to retrain employees, retrofitting the equipment and certifying the whole process again, Dubský adds.
Now the company is finalizing the preparation of the process for plating with zinc and nickel, which will replace the existing harmful cadmium so far.
Although they received the assignment from the manufacturer, they then had to customize the technology for equipment or chemicals that they use in Prague.
People from the Boeing will arrive, they will test the concept of the entire line.
Then we're going to show them the production.
They will also check our laboratory.
One set of samples will be tested themselves in their own laboratory, and then they compare the results with ours, approaching the way customers control them.
Remembrance is a long-term long-term exclusive contract for the Prague company.
Embarrassing embraries stand and falling today with the Prague plant.
For the A320 produces half of the total volume of doors.
Replacing Czech Latecoere is either impossible or very complex for Brazilian or European manufacturers.
However, this doesn’t mean he can say any price at the door.
Part of the contract there is a price curve.
It indicates how much one of the doors will cost after ten, fifty, hundred pieces produced.
The price per piece keeps dropping towards a certain limit value.
