The entire world is watching the U.S. Federal Reserve set to meet in two days on Wednesday and Thursday to make a crucial decision on whether to raise interest rates for the first time in nearly a decade.
A poll of 80 economists polled by Reuters has found that more than half of those who thought the Federal Reserve was going to make a decision now thinks they would stay idle for some time, leaving interest rates ranging from 0-0.25 per cent.
It's a historic event because it's gone years since the Fed lowered rates to zero.
For the first time, we are approaching a situation where the main central bank could be able to get off the zero bar and start the process of normalizing interest rates," says Angel Ubide, head of the Peterson Institute for International Economics.
The U.S. economy performed relatively well, there was a recovery in the form of adding trillions of dollars to the state account and creating only a low level of inflation.
However, the Fed cannot ignore a less rosy global perspective.
They have warned the markets to be ready for an increase, but the circumstances suggest that they also see great odds against this move.
The decision is due to be announced on Thursday 20:00 Cech.
