Experts Criticize Review of Electronic Published Public Health England
A government-fundered statement stating that e-cigarettes are 95 per cent safer than smoking has emerged from a meeting of 12 people from whom some have links to the tobacco industry, according to researchers.
Experts in the British Medical Journal (BMJ) have partnered with Lancet criticism of the evidence used in their e-cigarette report by Public Health England (PHE).
The PHE news agency published a "meastern" report last month and described it as a "comprehensive review of evidence."
However, several researchers questioned the extensiveness of the data and drew attention to the links between some experts and the tobacco industry.
An introductory journal Lancet last month attacked an "extremely flimsy base", at which PHE introduced its main conclusions.
Two other experts, who write for BMJ, have disputed the allegations allegedly "devealed on a rock or sand."
Martin McKee, professor of European public health at the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, and Simon Capewell, Professor of Clinical Epidemiology at the University of Liverpool, said: "The basic principle of public health is that politics should be based on evidence of effectiveness."
They argue that the public could be expected to be a PHE statement, which is "the current best estimate is that e-cigarettes are around 95% less harmful than smoking" are based on a detailed review of evidence and modeling.
In fact, this conclusion comes from a meeting of 12 people convened to draw up an analysis of a multi-criteria (MCDA) model to synthesize their views on the damage associated with various nicotine-containing products; the results of the negotiations were summarised in a research report.
McKee a Capewell reports that one sponsor of the meeting was a company called EuroSwiss Health, whose chief executive reportedly previously received funding from British American Tobacco for independent studies.
It has also endorsed BAT's public health credentials in the Sustainability report, he said.
One of the 12 attendees of the meeting granted funding from an e-cigarette maker, but not the funding allegedly obtained earlier from the tobacco company Philip Morris International, they add.
The reason for selecting the members of the panel is unavarnished, but they contain several well-familiar e-cigarette champions, some of whom also declude industrial funding in the newspaper.
Some others present at the meeting are unfamiliar for their professional knowledge in the fight against smoking.
Tobacco management also took part in the PHE meeting.
A message created in this group "exploitably admits," the missing "hard evidence of the harmfulness of most of the products on most criteria," McKee and Capewell wrote.
However, none of these references or restrictions is described in the PHE message.
McKee and Capewell argued that PHE claims that "so far there is no evidence that e-cigarettes act as a path to smoking for children or non-smokers," it was premature.
Professor Kevin Fenton, director of health and well-being at PHE, said the claims at BMJ had already been answered.
“Electronic cigarettes are significantly less harmful than smoking.”
One of two life-saving smokers will die because of his addiction.
All evidence suggests that the health risks posed by e-cigarettes are small in comparison, but we still need to examine long-term effects.
PHE has a clear obligation to inform the public of what the evidence is showing and also what they don't display, especially when there has been so much public turmoil about relative dangers compared to tobacco.
Nearly 80 000 people a year die from smoking-related diseases, and smoking costs NHS £2 billion a year.
By sharply emphasizing the current evidence - that although e-cigarettes aren't risk-free, they only bear a fraction of smoking damage - we're fulfilling our national competencies.
