"Sonogenetics" or control of brain cells using sound waves
This video demonstrates how audio impulses affect the direction the nematodes are moving.
Scientists have educated worms with a genetically-modified neural system that can control sound waves.
Minor nematodes change direction when they are affected by sound impulses that are inaudible to the human ear.
Pulses work by switching cells of genetically modified motor neurons, which are programmed to react to these ultrasonic waves.
Responsible: Scientists can read and record brain activity by means of light
The researchers stated that these worms are a great example of a new methodology called sonogenetic that uses ultrasound to activate brain, cardiac and muscular cells and activated outside the body.
Sreekanth Chalasani, a researcher from the Salk Institute in La Jolla, California, told the Guardian that this method could one day replace deep brain stimulation - an invasive procedure in which electrodes are injected into the patient's brain, stimulating the brain. The method is used, for example, to treat the symptoms of Parkinson's disease.
Nematodes do not usually respond to ultrasound, but Chalasani has found that the reaction occurs when they are placed in a fluid containing microscopic bubbles.
As he discovered, bubbles amplify ultrasonic waves, which then go through the body of worms.
Reinforcedrified ultrasonic waves act in TRP-4 ion ducts, which are located in the diaphragm of some worm cells.
Thanks to sound waves, these channels are opening and activating the cells with which they are associated, according to a report from the journal Nature Communications.
In order to control nematodes using ultrasonic waves, Chalasani genetically modified the worms so that some of their motor neurons contain TRP-4 ionic ducts.
When ultrasounds applied for such modified worms, sound waves were amplified by microbubbles and transferred to worms, where modified motor neurons switched on.
In many ways, the new method resembles optogenetics, a pioneering tool that allows scientists to switch on and off neurons using light.
Chalasani said that sonogenetics could have many advantages over this technique.
Contrary to light, which has to be disseminated with optical fiber to the desired place in the brain, ultrasound waves can penetrate tissues without restrictions and be sent directly to the brain.
"We believe that when we use gene therapy and therapeutic viruses, we can target human neurons and make them temporarily susceptible to ultrasound signals used in clinical treatment to treat neurological diseases," Chalasani said.
He added that the method would also find its application in the treatment of muscular cells and insulin-producing cells.
