No one has explained to me how I should keep accepted refugees in the country when they do not want to live in the Czech Republic.
Interior Minister Milan Chovanec (ČSSD) is complaining after talks with his European colleagues that other Czech states are pushing the Czech Republic into accepting quotas to redistribute refugees, but reject any discussion.
He also observes that so far no one has already explained to him how he should keep assigned refugees in the Czech Republic when they do not want to live with us anyway.
"We didn't get an answer, and that's why we didn't want quotas to be released from the final text," Chovanec told reporters.
Can you more specifically describe the position with which you came to the meetings of the Ministers of the Interior and the Foreign Affairs of the European Union?
We do not agree with mandatory quotas as a system that simply cannot function.
We can't talk about migrants as saus bags of salt or flies.
They are living beings who have some visions, they go somewhere, they have any relatives.
We wanted to hear from our colleagues how we are able to sustain migrants on the territory of the Member States if redistributed were to redistribute.
We can't imagine.
We didn't get an answer, and that's why we wanted the quotas to be omitted.
We have no kind of problem with other conclusions - functional hotspots, i.e. registration points, border protection, camps outside Europe, a trust fund for African countries and other things, we have no problem with that.
We've been talking about this for a year and we've said this is the way.
But to say that the quotas will solve everything?
We've recently dealt with for forty-three thousand, now it's a hundred and twenty thousand.
When will it be five hundred thousand?
And how do we keep those people in that territory?
Ninety-efive percent of all people who are crossing over us want to go to Germany.
Almost no one wants us, we just don't know how to hold them here.
Other foreigners, Ukrainians, Vietnamese and others who take the Czech Republic as a destination country have the ambition to come to us, they receive asylum in our country, but the interest of the people from this migration wave, i.e. from Syria and other countries, is limited close to zero.
You have pointed out that refugees don’t even want to join us, but we’ve already pledged to accept fifteen-hundred.
Wouldn’t it be possible to accept more in this way?
We have pledged to accept 1500 people, but on condition.
We used to say: functional hotspots, border protection, military operation.
Even the 1500 is that there will be 1,100 people with whom we will arrange an asylum procedure.
They will come to our detention facility and treat them in the same way as those we have intercepted.
It's a big question, and we're going to be debating with Europe whether we can detain people who will be registered in Italy and Greece or Hungary.
Because Europe behaves in such a way that if they have registration, they cannot be apprehended.
That is, they will come on our territory with registration, and if there is an agreement that is not yet, it may happen that we do not even keep those 1100 on the territory of the Czech Republic and they will go back to Germany upon arrival.
If we will be able to restrain them for the duration of the asylum procedure, we will arrange an asylum procedure.
If the citizens of Syria, Eritrea, almost 98 percent will gain international protection, according to European statistics.
Then they will get documents that will enable them to travel around Europe.
And I don't think these people will retire in target countries.
Then there are four hundred people who will resettly.
That's the second project that's going on so far, and that's where we've only progressed that we're debating with the churches about the possibility that they would be able to help them find employment, accommodation and so on.
But that's a very long run.
You said that other states were unwilling to discuss with us at ministerial meetings.
But isn't it that the Czech Republic only rejects quotas, but doesn't offer any solidarity move to unblock the discussion?
What exactly do you think is a solidarity move?
If the Federal Republic of Germany utters an exaggeration that all the migrants from Syria can go to them, the report spreads in an order of hours after all camps around Europe, but also in Turkey, Jordan.
It encourages another migration wave going to Germany because they just don’t want to join us.
We think that the way is the protection of the external borders - simply discouraging those migration waves, helping those in need, and separating economic migrants.
Defintively let them know they don't have a chance.
Last year alone, Fifty Thousand Kosovars came to Germany.
From safe Kosovo, where we pay all the saturation of this state, including the police, the courts, the public prosecutor's offices.
If we managed to detach them and not to accept them or accept them only according to the needs of the economies, the fifty per cent of what the social systems spend on them in vain today could go for refugees.
Compared to the very first day when Germany introduced controls, the number of detainees in the Czech Republic has leapfrowed.
Now it's eightei, the first day it was twelve.
As far as it should happen, in order to tighten border controls?
We were afraid of catching in the order of hundreds of people.
These are the numbers we can handle.
We still have several hundred vacancies in brick-and-mortar detention facilities.
Of course, all the people we deprive will be returned to Austria within seven days.
We're doing this as standard.
The Austrians are the ones who take these people away from us, it's complicated with Hungary, and the Greeks - that's a completely useless story.
So where's the tipping point?
Would it have to be a thousand a day?
I don't think it would have to be a few hundred a day.
Two-hundred, three-hundred-hundred-three-hundred-day-a-half, three-hundred-and-yield days, will exhaust our capacities so much that crisis plans will take place.
Then I agreed with the Prime Minister that he would immediately convene an extraordinary government.
He is very much interested in the whole thing, we are in telephone contact several times a day.
If the situation arises, the government can meet de facto immediately and in one resolution it would decide to strengthen the police on the army, let's say the customs administration, possibly the prison service.
The extreme option is a state of emergency, which Hungary has now declared in some places?
I don't think it can't come this far.
Recognize the fact that our position is different, we are a national state within Europe, we only have the Schengen border at the airport.
I don’t know what it would have to happen for this to happen on the territory of the Czech Republic, I don’t want to disseminate alarm information.
All the information we have from Moldavia, Serbia, Hungary suggests that the Hungarians will try to divert the migratory flow to Croatia and Slovenia, which would then mean a direct route to Austria.
The Czech Republic would be very frustrating, almost at all.
