The velocity on the highways will remain the same.
Acceleration at 150 km/h was rejected by the Chamber of Deputies.
It will not be possible to increase the maximum speed allowed up to 150 km/h on selected sections of highways under favorable conditions.
On Tuesday, the Chamber of Deputies ruled when it acceded to an amendment to the Senate, which rejected the speed increase.
An amendment to the law on roads will now go to the president.
President Miloš Zeman already stated at the end of April that he would veto the law allowing a hundred-and-tand-kilometre velocity.
Minister of Transport Dan Ťok (for ANO), according to which the Czech motorway network is not yet built for such a speed.
Ťok on Tuesday in front of MPs supported the senate version of the law.
The approved amendment will now, in accordance with the original government's proposal, allow an increase in speed from 90 to 110 kilometers per hour on selected first-class roads, especially four-lane ones.
The Chamber of Deputies first extended this option to highways, on which it would be possible to ride in selected sections at speeds of up to 150 km/h, but the Senate refused.
MEPs have now agreed to his suggestion.
Senators, with the consent of the Minister of Transport from the amendment, also exempted the authorization of the transport police to have the last word when modifying local roads, which are taken care of by municipalities.
The senatrix, approved by the Chamber of Deputies, also envisages that sewerage, water pipes or public lighting cables can be stored longitudinally under the surface of the roads.
Municipalities would thus save on the purchase of land.
Municipalities should also not be obliged to take care of road vegetation along local roads regardless of the owner of the relevant land.
The amendment is also intended to help improve the protection of the roads and make it easier for municipalities to the handling of towed-off vehicles, to which the landlords do not subscribe.
Newly, municipalities could suppose towed-to-refuelled vehicles that their landlords do not apply for within a six-month period after the towing notice.
Municipalities will have the opportunity to exclude transit freight traffic through their territory on second- and third-class roads under the threat of a fine even for forwarding companies.
The amendment also repeals the possibility to introduce electronic vignettes instead of existing paper vignancies, which will continue to be used after January 1 next year.
According to the government, this will save money for the introduction of an electronic system.
