#4 People Killed After Officers Shot Rubber Projectiles at Demonstrators in Nepal
Four people, including a four-year-old boy, were slaughtered in southern Nepal after police fissile fired rubber bullets into a mob of demonstrators against a proposal for a new state constitution, an official said on Wednesday.
Violence took place on Tuesday evening in Rupandehi, about 175 miles west of Kathmandu.
Bishnu Prasad Dhakal, the leading district officer, reports that more than 1,000 protesters gathered in front of a police station, throwing rocks and incendiary bombshells.
Pan Dhakal reports that the police used tear gas and then shot into the air before resorting to rubber bullets.
Twenty police officers and five civilians were injuvenated.
It wasn't immediately uncleaken why gummy projectiles, which are known for their ability to kill but are traditionally used as a less deadly alternative, apparently caused a high number of deaths.
This clash was the last week of violent demonstrations throughout the southern Plain of Nepal, where members of Madhesi ethnic group say they have set up a new province under the proposed new constitution and eased their political influence.
Further demonstrations in western Nepal have also been recorded by an Ethnic group of Tharus, which calls for the formation of its own state.
Since August 10, at least 40 people, including 11 police officers, have been slain in violent clashes.
when the four main political parties signed an agreement on the division of the country into provincials – a big step towards a new constitution, but a very contentious issue in Nepal, which has been centrally managed for centuries.
Work on a new constitution has been going on since 2008, but lawmakers have accelerated the trial of a devastating earthquake that killed thousands of people in April.
The legislators in Kathmandu this week voted on the provisions of the draft constitution, and officials say the new charter should be adopted by Sunday.
