The European Union has decided on Monday to collaborate ahead with the refugee problem: it will systematically classify migrants and asylum seekers as soon as they set foot on the old continent - especially in Greece, rather than arguing over the future distribution of refugees according to quotas on the basis of a quantity that is increasingly diverting to reality.
Six days after European Commission President Jean-Claude Juncker asked 25 of the 28 EU countries redistributed 160,000 refugees.
Before taking care of the final destination of refugees, it is first necessary to filter those who are already arriving today to solve the fate of those who are already moving inside the Schengen area.
"Efficial governance of the EU's external borders is necessary for managing migration," the text discussed by the ministers in Brussels on Monday said.
After the crisis of the common currency, the EU finds that it is in jeopardy in its next major integration project.
Germany may be amazed, but it doesn’t lose its decisiveness and increases the pressure.
It reinstates border controls, especially with Austria, but without blocking an influx of those seeking asylum in this country.
In doing so, the central power makes it clear that there is no vacancy possible in Europe without it.
Austria and Slovakia have joined this measure on Monday.
Poland, the Netherlands, Belgium and even France are also contemlining, albeit at varying intensity.
“Sorp to Hungary did this for a long time a long time.
These exceptional and temporary measures are admissuited according to Schengen rules.
That it is a dominatrient effect, he also sees a blind.
A key issue for Berlin and Paris now is the effective introduction of mandatory entrance controls in Greece, in southern Italy, as well as in Hungary for those who have already gone inland.
French Interior Minister Bernard Cazeneuve and his German collaborator Thomas de Maizière acted intensively during a private conversation with his three colleagues from Greece, Italy and Hungary.
This idea isn't new in any way, but it's resoluted.
According to unofficial statistics, two-thirds of the newly arrived refugees have already been registered in Italy and have been identified.
Budapest rejects the introduction of any entry point on its territory because they are more afraid than others, so that there will not be on its loess camps in which the fugitives piled up at its expense.
The real black hole remains Greece.
More than 200 thousand people landed on its islands near the Turkish coast this year, which is twice as much as it has recently arrived in Italy.
Most of them are true asylum seekers who came directly from Syria after going through overloaded camps in Lebanon, Turkey and Jordan.
It's a huge provocative hole.
Athens, which are still in financial disruption and awaiting a new government, put their hands in their laps.
This is the starting point for the Balkan route and a source of now common concerns for German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbán.
The Juncker Commission vows that the control points will take place as soon as possible, but it did not set a number or a date.
"Claims faced by Greece apply to the whole of Europe," EU interior ministers agreed on Monday for mobilization on this front.
It is therefore a matter of deploying the intervention teams of the European Frontex border control agencies in this country.
The EU aims to help Greece expand its capacity to receive asylum seekers and strengthen the oversight of the islands.
"There will be a need for reasonable financial resources," warns the text submitted to the ministers for approval.
From the point of view of the law, Athens will continue to have the last word.
The issue of the creation of real European border rallies has not been formally raised, but Europe is approaching.
The new outspoken situation of the twenty-five has thus shifted to the other sidelines the issue of mandatory quotas and 160 thousand refugees, which was already raised by the Juncker Commission and the European Parliament at the beginning of the year, strongly supported by Angela Merkel and French President François Hollande.
Hungary, Slovakia, the Czech Republic and Poland still do not even want to hear about it.
They consider it an attack on their solituation.
The number, but not an obligity, was preserved on Monday in a text approved by most twenty-wenty-wenties.
But as a political commitment.
In other words, the process is interrupted.
The only solid promise involves redistribution of 40,000 refugees over the next two years, which has already already been reached by an agreement was reached in June during a hectic EU summit, Le Figaro concludes.
