Student films will be dominated by Písek, an international festival begins.
More than 350 authors from 51 countries this year have enrolled their works in the competition of the 15th International Festival of Student Films in Písek this year.
Four dozen of them, which the committee composed of students and educators sent to the final, will be projected from 15 to 18 October in the cinema Portyč.
The best of them will be chosen by an international juror, festival spokesman Jakub Charvát said.
Every year the festival has a special theme that derives from the festival jingle.
It's a detective this year.
Richard Blumenfeld, the director of this year's galleries, plans to combine elements of television, film and theatrical.
"And, of course, we also have to solve the crime that has been committed in our trailer this year," Charvát said.
Visitors will see a selection of the most interesting of the student work - feature films, animated and documentary films.
They will be judged by an international juror who, for example, last year's winner, director Miklas Manneke, Spanish director Claudio Niubo and British producer Hemant Sharda.
Organizers of the festival, as every year, prepare an accompanying program.
Wattican Punk Ballet from Armenia, singer Ondřej Ruml, professional didgeridoo player Ondřej Smeykal, turbošanson band Poletíme?, playful band Zrní or, for example, Petra Hapková reciprocated Life at the bar.
The festival will also host two exhibitions of photographs.
The first one will be delivered by a graduate of the film school in Písek and a holder of prestigious awards, among which there is also this from World Press Photo 2014, Jana Ašenbrennerová.
The second exhibition will present the atmosphere that prevailed in Nepal after a tragic earthquake.
A camerawoman and documentary filmmaker Martin Čech will come to a discussion with the visitors, the mute film The Cameraman will be accompanied by Gerhard Gruber and the sand natives Brukner, Novotný and Hlach in the sculptor Jan Novotný will christen their new book 3 friends from the town of P.
This year we will also reminisce about Miroslav Ondříček, the Patriotic Patriotic of FAMO in Písek, who recently left us.
Our school and the festival are his legacy, which will continue to live.
"Young people from all over the world will continue to meet here, exchuding experiences with each other, and develop common ties as well as film craftsmanship," he said.
Pitching forum, where students can meet cinematic professionals, will certainly help to attend several work auditions at the same time to gain a job position.
The festival is associated with the establishment of the Private Higher Vocational School of Film in Písek.
Its founders, Vladana and Miloň Terčovi, organized the first year in 1997.
The sixth year was held once every two years.
In the event of the bankruptcy of CK, insurance companies are supposed to pay the demands of clients in the full amount.
Pojišt'ovnas should pay the claims of clients in the event of the bankruptcy of the travel agency in full.
This was decided by the Chamber of Deputies on Wednesday when it approved a revised amendment to the law on the conditions of doing business in tourism.
Travel agencies will be able, according to the sub-proposal heading to the Senate, they will be able to resemble insurance against the bankruptcy with a banking guarantee.
Pojišt'ovnas do not agree with the obligation to pay the claims of their clients even over the agreed limit.
The Ministry of Regional Development insists on the regulation, arguing the protection of the consumer.
Pojišt'ovnas have already been threatened to terminate the insuring of travel agencies against the crash when the amendment applies.
Travel Agencies have also criticized the proposal.
Without insurrements, they will either have to quit or go abroad.
Minister for Regional Development Karla Šlechtová (for ANO) pointed out that cases where insurance companies did not pay full reimbursements are not European-comformed.
She drew attention to this year's decision of the Constitutional Court, which confirmed that the client is entitled to a full refund regardless of the insurance limit.
Pojišt'ovnas will receive sufficient information about the management of travel agencies and they will be able to determine the right limit, Šlechtová emphasized.
"We appreciate an effort to increase consumer protection, but it must have such a limit to be feasible," said Miroslav Kalousek, vice-chairman of TOP 09.
According to him, the banking guarantee will not work, because no one will give it indefinite.
"Travel agencies will have to terminate their activities or move abroad," Kalousek warned.
Tomio Okamura (SPD), a member of the Presidium Association of Czech Travel Agencies and Agencies, disagreed with Kalousek’s concerns on the basis of a survey among CK member states.
"Stop showing us a lobbing of insurance companies," he said.
Okamura does not consider the statements of insurance companies to be relevant, according to him, there is no their official written statement in this sense.
However, they find out whether the travel agencies will complaculg, which, according to Okamura, is not a problem.
Jana Fischerová (ODS), who referred the opinion of a compensatory Association of the Travel Agency of the Czech Republic, and her party colleague Petr Bendl, who talked about lubrication of stairs to Czech travel agencies, also warned against the appointment.
According to Fischer, travel agencies will not be able to take out insurance.
"Consumer protection works," Fischer said.
Her proposal to release the disputed passage was not approved by the House.
Vojtěch Filip (KSČM) did not march with the proposal to return the draft to the committee due to an alleged contradiction to the Civil Code.
Filip drew attention to the travel agencies, which, according to him, could endanger consumers.
Due to the verdict, Martina Berdychová (ANO), the Rapporteur of the draft Martina Berdychová (ANO) withdrew her proposal, which would limit the liability of insurance companies in cases where they could not have known that the indemnity did not cover the entire risk.
In the amendment, there will be no originally proposed minimum four-million-dollar insurance limit for travel agencies that offer tours by air transport, and a million-dollar limit for offices selling, for example, bus tours.
The boundary will remain at 30 per cent of the planned sales from the sale of tours.
However, the limit of inefficiencies will have to be such that in the event of the bankruptcy of the travel agency it covers all the demands of customers.
Even after the modifications, the standard stipulates that travel agencies will now have to specify which travel agency is organizing for the tickets sold.
Travel Agencies will have to provide insurance companies, among other things, data on the management and the number of trips before concluding the contract.
Approved an amendment to the Committee on Public Administration also envisages further regular information of insurance companies.
Travel Agencies will have to provide them every month with data on the number of passengers and tours sold, the total price of the tours sold, and the amount of advances and revenues for tours.
If the Ministry of Regional Development finds that the travel agency did not meet any of the legal obligations to the insurance company, it will invite it under the draft for remediation or will be able to prohibit it from concluding new contracts in the event that a breach of duty could jeopardize the interests of customers.
The amendment also aims to re-addaid the financing of the mountain service.
The Ministry of Regional Development will determine the amount of the subsidy to the mountain service on the basis of its application, which it will always be filed until the end of April for the next year.
The application will contain a report on the use of state money in the past year, the financial plan of expenses and the outlook for the next two years.
According to the association, however, the petitioner of the law did not conclute the situation of what will happen if the insurance company terminates the contract.
It won’t be allowed to organize tours and clients who have purchased a trip before expelling the insurrection will lose it, according to the association.
In such a case CK suffers a significant loss and may even go bankrupt, even though its management was in good shape.
One of the ways travel agencies can cope with such a situation will be a lawsuit against the state for compensation.
Another option will be to go abroad and to use much softer conditions abroad, and on our market then we will act only as agencies, said Vice-Chairman of the association Jan Pope.
According to him, the proposal of the amendment also contains the institute of a bank guarantee, but so unpremeditated that it can not be carried out in practice at all, because the bank guarantee can not amount to an unlimited amount.
In addition, according to the association, a change in the current practice is de facto useless.
Within two years it will be Necessary to revise the whole law due to the new EU directive.
A total of 942 active insured travel agencies now operate on the Czech market.
This year, there were only three CKs terminated their activities.
Domestic travel agencies annually sell tours worth about twenty billion CZK.
Merkel: We need to improve conditions in the Middle East.
The European Union needs to work to improve humanitarian conditions at refugee camps in Jordan and Lebanon in order to curb the arrival of refugees into Europe.
German Chancellor Angela Merkel announced this after talks with German provincial prime ministers.
At the same time, it once again appealed for the distribution of refugees who had already arrived in the EU among the twenty-eight member countries.
We need to help the countries where the fuganciers are leaving, in which they are staying after leaving their homeland.
I await a signal from the European Union that we will push for dignified conditions in refugee camps, such as in Jordan and Lebanon, the German chancellor said, according to which such a signal could also include higher financial assistance or closer EU cooperation with international humanitarian organizations.
"Federal countries also support the German government to promote a fair distribution of the weight of the refugee crisis into European countries," Merkel said.
Brandenburg's Prime Minister Dietmar Woidke said at a press conference that he expects real solidarity from all EU states.
The distribution of refugees in the EU on the basis of quotas has long been rejected, among other things, by the Czech Republic, among other things.
Merkel and Woiske also voted in favour of speeding up asylum procedures in Germany.
People need to know as soon as they have a chance to stay.
It is also necessary for the authorities, so that they can start integrating foreigners more quickly, the Prime Minister of Brandenburg said.
According to him, it is also necessary that the majority of the money that Germany spends on the care of the refugees serves precisely for the integration of those who have a chance of obtaining asylum.
There will be a need for more teachers for refugees to cope with German.
It is a big challenge for our country, but I believe that if the refugees can be integrant, they can become beneficial for us, he said.
