"American Ninja Warrior has the first winner"
Professional mountaineer and pikolík Isaac Caldiero became the first contestant to win the American Ninja Warrior competition on Monday and took home one million dollars.
In the competition, which just finished her seventh season, none of the competitors managed to overcome all the obstacles on the track.
During the final of this year's season, two men made it to the last round, Caldiero and cinematographer Geoff Britten.
The last impediment was a 30-second scoundrel after more than a 220 m long rope.
Britten graduated in the first place at the set-limit and became the first competitor to surpass the debilitating 4 sections in the American Ninja Warrior, but Caldiero surpassed it by a full three-season and won the competition.
Caldiero gave an interview for news! News immediately after its hard-earned victory and it was clear that he was still shocked at the $1-million-dollar win.
"It's an amazing feeling. I've never earned more than $10,000 a year in my life," Isaac E! said in response to his win.
"All of a sudden... I can't even imagine what it's going to be like now."
Executive producer Kent Weed gave an interview to The Hollywood Reporter about the course of the final and why Britten doesn't get any reward despite the fact that he's become the first competitor who completed the track.
According to the rules, the money belongs to whoever completes the track the fastest.
“If the track is attended by multiple competitors, the win will get the one with the best time,” Weed said.
Even if winning the main win would change his life, Britten is happy with his performance.
"I don't think he'd have grudgement.
He's glad he managed to achieve what he's done.
He received a warm welcome from the fans.
He's a great man and he likes his family.
He's a hero for his children and a woman."
Geoff told me: "If there should have been someone who could have beaten me, I'm glad it was Isaac."
And I don't think, if I've won, Isaac would have felt the same.
America's Ninja Warrior will continue its eight-season, which will begin shooting in the spring of 2016.
