Xi calls for an opening economy to a wider-glomeer
It is necessary to rebuild the economy into the wider world to boost growth, President Xi Jinping told the group, tasked with Tuesday's governance reform.
"China should be involved in tempting foreign investment and professional knowledge and improving open policies," he said.
Promoting openings on nascent reforms will add new impetus and vitality, it will also provide a new scope for economic growth, Xi says.
The leading group has adopted a number of directives, including a negative list of regulatory market access, easing border control policy, support for state-owned enterprises (SOE) to absorb private capital and reorganisation of the registration residency system.
The statement states that a negative list will be drawn up, in which sectors and businesses that are out-of-in-exchange limits are identified.
This system will be progressively tested and improved through test programs.
This step is significant because it will give the market a greater role in allocating resources, securing the legal business environment and making the market more open, the statement states.
The government will also loosen controls on the powers it delegates to companies and makes sure that the companies themselves will decide how to manage their business.
It is also stated in the statement that China will allow the border areas to discover new models of cross-border economic cooperation and new mechanisms to promote regional growth.
In an quest to unbuild a state-sponsored sector, more areas will be open to private investment.
"The government will not change its policy vis-à-vis foreign investment and will protect the legal interests of companies that are financed from abroad and will provide them with better services," it said.
In addition, it will be easier for foreigners to apply for a permanent residency permit or a "green card", by optimizing the requirements and simplifying the application process.
Ensuitance of the rights of lawyers practised and develop professional judiciary and the public prosecutor was also discussed at the meeting.
The meeting was also attended by Prime Minister Li Keqiang and leaders Liu Yunshan and Zhang Gaoli, according to a statement issued after the meeting.
There may be new inpatient ward departments in the Karlovy Vary region.
According to Jakub Pánik (ČSSD), Deputy Governor of the Karlovy Vary Region (ČSSD), the simultaneous capacity of inpatient wards of two regional hospitals and two private hospitals in the region is sufficient, and partial adjustments should be done on the basis of an agreement between departments of individual hospitals.
However, there are also opportunities to expand care in the county.
For example, the Department of Surgery Departments could create a one-Daiday Surgery center, where, according to the set concept, procedures of surgical disciplines that do not require hospitalization for more than 24 hours would be carried out.
It is not yet unclear in which specific hospitals focused on one-day-on-Day Surgery.
In turn, a laser center with the possibility of using a laser technique for skin diseases could be established at the skin department of the Karlovy Vary hospital.
The concept also recommended the emergence of an apaltic center that would take care of patients with brain disorders who are in a vegetative state.
Such a center should be established centrally and should be an integral part of the anaesthesia-Resuscitation department.
There is also the possibility of concentrating such a facility in the hospital Ostrov, where similar care already exists.
Another inpatient capacity, which appears insufficient in the Karlovy Vary region, are the beds of follow-up care, especially the beds of Long-Term Wealth Care (LDN), whose capacity in Nejdek is insufficient and, above all, it should be closely linked to acute care beds.
Part of this device should act as rehabilitation beds and part as a classic LDN beds, recommends a concept.
As he points out, after-care beds are lacking mainly in the Karlovy Vary hospital.
There you could take advantage of abandoned buildings after centralization.
Last but not least in the Karlovy Vary region, there are absences social health beds focusing on the treatment of Alzheimer's disease.
There is a shortage of these beds in the whole country, therefore it could also serve for other counties a so-called Alzheimercentrum.
According to the recommendations, hospitals should also prepare for the introduction of so-called follow-and-tall beds, for which the funds of European subsidies will be released.
"These are beds, which will build on the fields of superspecialized care and will form a space for the translation of patients after the procedure, for example, after cardiosurgery, neurosurgical, transplantation or other procedures," describes the regional concept of inpatient care.
New services could also be offered in the area of psychiatric care.
